@unpublished{ralston2025theillusionkindpredication,
  abstract = {\ast \ast This is a draft of a paper that was presented at Sinn und Bedeutung 30. Please share any comments or feedback with me via email: tomgjralston \textquotesingle at\textquotesingle  gmail.com\ast \ast 


Predicates such as \textquotesingle are extinct\textquotesingle  or \textquotesingle are widespread\textquotesingle  have long been assumed to apply only to kinds. I present evidence that the class of predicates typically described as kind-level are \textquotesingle numerous\textquotesingle -type collective predicates, based on their interaction with \textquotesingle all\textquotesingle  and their failure to exhibit homogeneity effects. I argue that this undermines the principal semantic motivation for analysing their bare plural arguments as kind-denoting. Instead, I argue that their arguments are
type 𝑒 expressions and that plural kind predication is just plural predication. I give a compositional semantics for bare plurals and explain differences between bare and definite plurals in terms of the situation argument that can restrict their domain.},
